### Upper and lower case (R23).

These were the last two string methods the backend refused, and the reason
was never that they are hard -- it is that getting them nearly right is
worse than refusing. A version agreeing on ASCII and diverging on Greek
would make `halka run` and `halka build` two different languages for anyone
not writing in English.

The tables are generated from the same JavaScript the interpreter runs on
(tools/gen-unicase.mjs), so the two engines agree by construction rather
than by inspection. That was checked exhaustively once, over all 1,112,064
code points, by summing every mapping in both engines and comparing: they
match. What is here is the part worth reading -- the cases that are not a
simple one-to-one shift, and a sweep dense enough to catch a table that
drifted.
###
main(),
    # The easy half.
    say "hello".upper(),
    say "WORLD".lower(),
    say "MiXeD".upper(),
    say "MiXeD".lower(),
    say "".upper(),
    say "123 !?".upper(),

    # Mappings that grow. German sharp s becomes two letters, so the result
    # is longer than the input -- which is why the C side measures before it
    # fills rather than assuming the length.
    say "Straße".upper(),
    say "Straße".upper().length,
    say "ﬁ".upper(),
    say "ﬄ".upper(),
    say "ﬄ".upper().length,

    # And one that grows going *down*: a capital I with a dot above becomes
    # an i plus a combining dot.
    say "İ".lower().length,

    # Final sigma, the one place the answer depends on where the character
    # sits rather than on what it is. A capital sigma ending a word becomes
    # the final form; anywhere else it becomes the ordinary one.
    say "ΑΣ".lower(),
    say "ΑΣΒ".lower(),
    say "Σ".lower(),
    say "ΟΔΟΣ".lower(),
    say "ΟΔΟΣ ΤΗΣ".lower(),

    # An accent between the sigma and the letter before it is ignored when
    # deciding, which is what makes the rule more than "is the next
    # character a letter".
    say "ΆΣ".lower(),

    # Alphabets that case in blocks, which is what the run table is for.
    say "ΑΒΓΔΕΖΗΘ".lower(),
    say "абвгде".upper(),
    say "ԱԲԳԴ".lower(),
    say "ⓐⓑⓒ".upper(),

    # Outside the basic plane, where a code point is four bytes.
    say "𐐀".lower(),
    say "𐒰".lower(),
    say "𞤀".lower(),

    # Round trips that are not round trips, which is Unicode being itself
    # rather than anything going wrong.
    say "ß".upper().lower(),
    say "İ".lower().upper(),

    # A sweep over the dense region, reduced to one number. A table that
    # drifted by a single entry changes it.
    let up: 0,
    let lo: 0,
    for cp in 0..8192,
        if cp < 55296 or cp > 57343,
            let s: "{cp as char}",
            for c in s.upper().chars(),
                up: (up + c as int) % 1000000007,
            for c in s.lower().chars(),
                lo: (lo + c as int) % 1000000007,
    say up,
    say lo
